% ============================================================
% Introduction
% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:healing-intro}

Compiler optimization pipelines are complex, fragile systems.  A modern
optimizing compiler contains hundreds of passes, each of which may fail for
various reasons: assertion failures, internal compiler errors, timeouts, memory
exhaustion, or incorrect transformations detected by translation validation
(\cref{ch:translation-validation}).  When a pass fails, the traditional response
is to abort the compilation, leaving the user with an error message and no
compiled code.

\emph{Self-healing compiler pipelines} take a different approach: when a pass
fails, the compiler automatically recovers by falling back to a safer
configuration, skipping the failed pass, or trying an alternative optimization
strategy.  The goal is to ensure that compilation always succeeds, even in the
presence of failures, while still producing high-quality code.

This concept draws inspiration from self-healing systems in other domains:
fault-tolerant distributed systems, self-healing networks, and resilient
software architectures.  The key principles are:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Failure detection:} Recognize when a pass has failed.
\item \textbf{Automatic recovery:} Respond to failures without user intervention.
\item \textbf{Graceful degradation:} Produce the best possible output even when
      some passes fail.
\item \textbf{Learning:} Improve the pipeline's behavior based on past failures.
\end{itemize}

In this chapter, we develop the theory and practice of self-healing compiler
pipelines, from failure detection (\cref{sec:healing-detection}) through
recovery strategies (\cref{sec:healing-recovery}) to learning from failures
(\cref{sec:healing-learning}) and monitoring (\cref{sec:healing-monitoring}).

% ============================================================
% Detecting Optimization Failures
% ============================================================
\section{Detecting Optimization Failures}\label{sec:healing-detection}

\subsection{Types of Failures}\label{sec:healing-failure-types}

Compiler optimization passes can fail in several ways:

\begin{definition}[Failure Classification]\label{def:failure-class}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Hard failures:} The pass crashes (segfault, assertion failure,
      unhandled exception).  The IR may be in an inconsistent state.
\item \textbf{Soft failures:} The pass detects a problem and reports it
      (e.g., ``unable to vectorize this loop'').  The IR is unchanged.
\item \textbf{Silent failures:} The pass produces incorrect output (a
      miscompilation).  The failure is not detected by the pass itself.
\item \textbf{Resource failures:} The pass exceeds a resource limit (time,
      memory).  The IR may be partially modified.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Failure Detection Mechanisms}\label{sec:healing-detection-mech}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Assertions:} Internal assertions check the IR's consistency
      before and after each pass.  An assertion failure indicates a hard
      failure.
\item \textbf{IR verification:} A verification pass checks the IR's well-formedness
      (e.g., SSA dominance, type correctness) after each transformation.
\item \textbf{Translation validation:} A validator checks that the pass
      preserved the program's semantics (\cref{ch:translation-validation}).
\item \textbf{Timeouts:} A watchdog timer kills passes that exceed a time limit.
\item \textbf{Memory limits:} A memory limiter prevents passes from exhausting
      available memory.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Consistent State Recovery}\label{sec:healing-consistent}

When a hard failure occurs, the IR may be in an inconsistent state.  Recovery
requires restoring the IR to a consistent state:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Pre-pass snapshot:} Save the IR before each pass; restore on
      failure.  This is the approach used by transactional transformations
      (\cref{ch:transactional}).
\item \textbf{Verification-based:} If the IR fails verification, re-parse from
      the last known-good state.
\item \textbf{Checkpoint-based:} Periodically checkpoint the IR; restore from
      the last checkpoint on failure.
\end{itemize}

% ============================================================
% Automatic Fallback Strategies
% ============================================================
\section{Automatic Fallback Strategies}\label{sec:healing-recovery}

\subsection{Pass Skipping}\label{sec:healing-skip}

The simplest recovery strategy is to \emph{skip} the failed pass and continue
with the next pass.  This is appropriate when:
\begin{itemize}
\item The failed pass is not critical (its optimization is nice-to-have, not
      essential).
\item Subsequent passes do not depend on the failed pass's output.
\end{itemize}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Pass Skipping with Fallback}\label{alg:healing-skip}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Pass pipeline $P = [p_1, \ldots, p_n]$, IR state $S$
\Ensure Optimized IR state
\For{$i = 1$ to $n$}
  \State $S_{\mathit{saved}} \gets \mathit{snapshot}(S)$
  \State \textbf{try}
    \State $S \gets p_i(S)$
    \State $\mathit{verify}(S)$
  \State \textbf{catch} \textit{Failure}
    \State Log failure: $p_i$ failed on $S_{\mathit{saved}}$
    \State $S \gets S_{\mathit{saved}}$ \Comment{Restore pre-pass state}
    \State \textbf{continue} \Comment{Skip to next pass}
  \State \textbf{end try}
\EndFor
\State \Return $S$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Optimization Level Reduction}\label{sec:healing-level}

A more aggressive strategy is to reduce the optimization level.  If passes at
\texttt{-O3} fail, retry at \texttt{-O2}; if \texttt{-O2} fails, retry at
\texttt{-O1}; and so on.

\begin{definition}[Optimization Level Cascade]\label{def:opt-cascade}
An \emph{optimization level cascade} is a sequence of optimization
configurations $C_0 \supset C_1 \supset \cdots \supset C_n$ where each $C_i$
is a subset of the passes in $C_{i-1}$.  On failure at level $C_i$, the
compiler falls back to $C_{i+1}$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Alternative Pass Selection}\label{sec:healing-alternative}

Some optimizations can be achieved by multiple passes.  If one pass fails, an
alternative pass can be tried:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Register allocation:} If graph-coloring allocation fails, fall
      back to linear-scan allocation.
\item \textbf{Instruction selection:} If tree-pattern matching fails, fall back
      to instruction scheduling with a simpler selector.
\item \textbf{Loop optimization:} If polyhedral optimization fails, fall back
      to classical loop transformations.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Partial Optimization}\label{sec:healing-partial}

For function-level failures, the compiler can optimize some functions and leave
others unoptimized:

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Partial Optimization}\label{alg:healing-partial}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Module $M$ with functions $f_1, \ldots, f_n$, pass pipeline $P$
\Ensure Optimized module
\For{each $f_i \in M$}
  \State \textbf{try}
    \State $f_i \gets \mathit{optimize}(f_i, P)$
  \State \textbf{catch} \textit{Failure}
    \State Log: $f_i$ could not be optimized
    \State $f_i \gets \mathit{compile\_unoptimized}(f_i)$
  \State \textbf{end try}
\EndFor
\State \Return $M$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

