\section{Learning from Failures}\label{sec:healing-learning}

\subsection{Failure Databases}\label{sec:healing-db}

A \emph{failure database} records all pass failures, including the pass that
failed, the IR state at the time of failure, and the recovery action taken.
This database can be used to:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Identify recurring failures:} If a pass frequently fails on
      certain IR patterns, the pass can be fixed or the pattern can be avoided.
\item \textbf{Tune the pipeline:} Adjust the pass ordering or configuration to
      avoid known failure modes.
\item \textbf{Train predictive models:} Use machine learning to predict which
      passes are likely to fail on a given IR, and proactively skip them.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Predictive Failure Avoidance}\label{sec:healing-predict}

Given a failure database, the compiler can learn to \emph{predict} failures:

\begin{definition}[Failure Prediction]\label{def:failure-pred}
A \emph{failure predictor} is a function $F : \mathit{IR} \times \mathit{Pass} \to [0, 1]$
that estimates the probability that pass $p$ will fail on IR state $S$.
\end{definition}

The predictor can be implemented using:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Rule-based:} Hand-crafted rules (e.g., ``skip vectorization if
      the loop has more than 100 basic blocks'').
\item \textbf{Statistical:} Frequency-based estimates from the failure database.
\item \textbf{Machine learning:} A classifier trained on features of the IR
      and pass configuration.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Adaptive Pass Ordering}\label{sec:healing-adaptive}

The pass ordering can be adapted based on past failures:
\begin{itemize}
\item Move reliable passes earlier in the pipeline.
\item Move failure-prone passes later, after the IR has been simplified.
\item Skip passes that have a high predicted failure probability.
\end{itemize}

