% Redundant Optimization Paths
% ============================================================
\section{Redundant Optimization Paths}\label{sec:healing-redundant}

\subsection{Multiple Optimization Strategies}\label{sec:healing-multi-strategy}

A self-healing pipeline can maintain \emph{multiple optimization strategies},
each optimized for different characteristics of the input:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Aggressive strategy:} Maximizes optimization, accepts higher risk
      of failure.
\item \textbf{Conservative strategy:} Minimizes risk of failure, accepts lower
      optimization.
\item \textbf{Balanced strategy:} Intermediate risk and reward.
\end{itemize}

The pipeline starts with the aggressive strategy and falls back to more
conservative strategies on failure.

\subsection{Parallel Redundant Execution}\label{sec:healing-parallel}

For critical compilations (e.g., safety-critical code), the pipeline can run
multiple strategies in parallel and compare the results:

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Redundant Optimization with Cross-Checking}\label{alg:healing-redundant}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require IR state $S$, strategies $T_1, T_2$
\Ensure Optimized IR or failure
\State $S_1 \gets T_1(S)$ \Comment{Parallel}
\State $S_2 \gets T_2(S)$ \Comment{Parallel}
\If{$\mathit{validate}(S_1) \land \mathit{validate}(S_2)$}
  \If{$\mathit{equivalent}(S_1, S_2)$}
    \State \Return $\mathit{better}(S_1, S_2)$
  \Else
    \State Report: strategies disagree (potential miscompilation)
    \State \Return $S$ \Comment{Conservative: no optimization}
  \EndIf
\ElsIf{$\mathit{validate}(S_1)$}
  \State \Return $S_1$
\ElsIf{$\mathit{validate}(S_2)$}
  \State \Return $S_2$
\Else
  \State \Return $S$ \Comment{Both failed}
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

% ============================================================
% Graceful Degradation
% ============================================================
\section{Graceful Degradation}\label{sec:healing-graceful}

\subsection{Quality Metrics}\label{sec:healing-quality}

A self-healing pipeline should track the \emph{quality} of the output code:

\begin{definition}[Compilation Quality]\label{def:quality}
\emph{Compilation quality} is a vector of metrics:
\[
Q = (\mathit{correctness}, \mathit{performance}, \mathit{code\_size}, \mathit{compile\_time})
\]
where $\mathit{correctness} \in \{0, 1\}$ indicates whether the output is
correct, and the other metrics are continuous values.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Degradation Policies}\label{sec:healing-policies}

A \emph{degradation policy} specifies how the pipeline should respond to
failures:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Correctness-first:} Never produce incorrect code.  If a pass
      might produce incorrect code, skip it.
\item \textbf{Performance-first:} Maximize performance even at the cost of
      longer compile times.  Retry failed passes with more resources.
\item \textbf{Time-first:} Minimize compile time.  Skip failed passes without
      retrying.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{User Notification}\label{sec:healing-notification}

When the pipeline degrades (e.g., skips a pass or reduces optimization level),
the user should be notified:

\begin{verbatim}
  warning: pass 'loop-vectorize' failed on function 'compute_fft'
           (assertion failure in LCSSA verification)
           falling back to unoptimized code for this function
           performance may be degraded
\end{verbatim}

