\section{Monitoring and Observability}\label{sec:healing-monitoring}

\subsection{Pipeline Telemetry}\label{sec:healing-telemetry}

A self-healing pipeline should emit \emph{telemetry}---structured data about
its behavior---for monitoring and analysis:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Pass execution log:} Which passes ran, how long they took,
      whether they succeeded.
\item \textbf{Failure log:} Which passes failed, why, and what recovery action
      was taken.
\item \textbf{Quality metrics:} The quality of the output code (estimated
      performance, code size, etc.).
\item \textbf{Resource usage:} Memory and time consumed by each pass.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Dashboards and Alerts}\label{sec:healing-dashboards}

Telemetry data can be visualized in dashboards that show:
\begin{itemize}
\item The health of the compilation pipeline (pass success rate, failure rate).
\item Trends over time (is the failure rate increasing?).
\item Specific failure patterns (which passes fail most often?).
\end{itemize}

Alerts can be configured to notify developers when:
\begin{itemize}
\item The failure rate exceeds a threshold.
\item A new type of failure is observed.
\item The output quality degrades below a threshold.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Continuous Improvement}\label{sec:healing-continuous}

The telemetry data feeds back into the failure database, enabling continuous
improvement:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Monitor pipeline health.
\item Identify recurring failures.
\item Fix the root causes (in the passes or in the pipeline configuration).
\item Verify that the fixes reduce the failure rate.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{insight}
Self-healing compiler pipelines embody a fundamental shift in how we think about
compiler reliability.  Rather than treating failures as catastrophic events that
halt compilation, we treat them as expected occurrences that the pipeline is
designed to handle gracefully.  This is analogous to the shift from
fault-intolerant to fault-tolerant distributed systems: the system is designed
to continue operating even when individual components fail.  The key insight is
that \emph{partial optimization is better than no optimization}: a program
compiled with 90\% of the intended optimizations is far more useful than a
program that fails to compile.
\end{insight}

\begin{historical}
The concept of self-healing compiler pipelines is relatively new, but its
components have deep roots.  Translation validation (\cref{ch:translation-validation})
provides the failure detection mechanism.  Transactional transformations
(\cref{ch:transactional}) provide the rollback and recovery mechanism.  The idea
of optimization level cascades is implicit in compilers that support multiple
optimization levels (\texttt{-O0}, \texttt{-O1}, \texttt{-O2}, \texttt{-O3}).
The integration of these ideas into a unified self-healing framework is an
active area of research, driven by the increasing complexity of compiler
optimization pipelines and the growing demand for compiler reliability.
\end{historical}

% ============================================================
% Summary
% ============================================================
\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:healing-summary}

Self-healing compiler pipelines improve the reliability and robustness of
compilation by automatically detecting and recovering from optimization failures.
The key principles are failure detection, automatic recovery, graceful
degradation, and continuous learning.

Key open challenges include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Balancing recovery and quality:} Ensuring that recovery does not
      sacrifice too much code quality.
\item \textbf{Predicting failures:} Building accurate failure predictors that
      can proactively avoid failures.
\item \textbf{Formal guarantees:} Proving that the recovery mechanism preserves
      program semantics.
\item \textbf{Integration with existing compilers:} Retrofitting self-healing
      capabilities into production compilers.
\end{itemize}
